# Performance Rules – Hot Paths

## Object Pool Pattern
Use `sync.Pool` for `DecisionContext`, `FeatureMap`, and `RuleResult`:

```go
// ✅ GOOD
var ctxPool = sync.Pool{New: func() any { return &DecisionContext{} }}
ctx := ctxPool.Get().(*DecisionContext)
ctx.Reset()          // always reset before use
defer ctxPool.Put(ctx)

// ❌ BAD – allocates on every request
ctx := &DecisionContext{}
```

## Parallel Feature Fetch
```go
// ✅ GOOD – errgroup with deadline
g, gctx := errgroup.WithContext(ctx)
g.Go(func() error { return fetchRedis(gctx, &result.UserFeatures) })
g.Go(func() error { return fetchDevice(gctx, &result.DeviceFeatures) })
_ = g.Wait()  // degraded result on partial timeout is acceptable

// ❌ BAD – sequential fetch wastes 3× latency
```

## Map Pre-allocation
```go
// ✅ GOOD
features := make(FeatureMap, 32)   // known approximate size

// ❌ BAD
features := FeatureMap{}
```

## CGO Isolation for ONNX
ONNX inference via CGO MUST run in a dedicated pool:
```go
// Submitted to onnxPool (chan), never called from arbitrary goroutine
// Reason: CGO locks OS thread; mixing with goroutine scheduler causes latency spikes
```

## GC Tuning (set in main.go)
```go
runtime.GOMAXPROCS(runtime.NumCPU())
// Set via env: GOGC=200 GOMEMLIMIT=1GiB
// Do NOT hardcode; let ops configure per environment
```

## Benchmark Requirements
Every new evaluator or fetcher MUST have a `BenchmarkXxx` function.
Target: rule eval < 500µs/op for 100 rules, feature fetch < 10ms P99.
